,"sub":"kennethtemple.com","note":"Cajun and Creole recipes from a New Orleans-based cook and video creator.","url":"https://kennethtemple.com/recipes/cajun-creole/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Louisiana Creole cuisine","links":[["Eggs Sardou","dish/eggs-sardou"],["New Orleans hot sausage","dish/new-orleans-hot-sausage"],["Oysters Bienville","dish/oysters-bienville"],["Oysters Rockefeller","dish/oysters-rockefeller"],["Red beans and rice","dish/red-beans-and-rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b8/BrennansFromCourthouseStepsMay07.jpg/500px-BrennansFromCourthouseStepsMay07.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"see above","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrennansFromCourthouseStepsMay07.jpg"}},"dish/banh-giay":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/banh-giay","n":"Bánh giầy","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Vietnamese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bánh giầy is a Vietnamese traditional cake. Bánh giầy is a white, flat, and round glutinous rice cake. They are wrapped in cut pieces of banana leaves. They are usually served with a type of Vietnamese sausage giò lụa. Bánh giầy can be fried to a thin crispy golden crust or be eaten with giò lụa. Another variation is called bánh giầy đậu, where ground boiled mung bean (đậu xanh)—salted or sweetened—is stuffed inside. It is very similar to other Asian glutinous rice cakes like Japanese mochi, Korean tteok or Chinese lo mai chi and ciba (糍粑)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-banh-giay"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A1nh_gi%E1%BA%A7y"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","ref":"site/vietworldkitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and 