"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5f/Shrimp_gumbo.jpg/500px-Shrimp_gumbo.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"jons2 at pdphoto.org","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrimp_gumbo.jpg"}},"ingredient/brachyura":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brachyura","n":"Brachyura","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Brachyura is an infraorder of decapod crustaceans comprising the true crabs. They typically have a very short projecting tail-like abdomen, usually hidden entirely under the thorax. Their exoskeleton is often thickened and hard. They generally have five pairs of legs, and the frontmost pair end in chelae, which function as pincers. They are present in all the world's oceans, in freshwater, and on land, often hiding themselves in small crevices or burrowing into sediment. True crabs are omnivores, feeding on a variety of food, including a significant proportion of algae, as well as detritus and other invertebrates. True crabs are widely consumed by humans as food, with over 1.5 million tonnes caught annually."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brachyura"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brachyura"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Gumbo","dish/gumbo"]]],["Cajun cuisine","cuisine/cajun-cuisine",[["Gumbo","dish/gumbo"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Filé powder","ingredient/file-powder"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Ostreidae","ingredient/ostreidae"],["Roux","ingredient/roux"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f9/Brachyura_montage.jpg/500px-Brachyura_montage.jpg","w":500,"h":270,"by":"Biopics Dan Boone Honguyenseo Bernard Picton Biopics Philippe Boujon Splette Nosferattus…","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brachyura_montage.jpg"}},"dish/krupuk-kulit":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/krupuk-kulit","n":"Krupuk kulit","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Krupuk kulit is a traditional Indonesian cattle skin krupuk (cracker). It is traditionally made from the soft inner skin of cattle (cow or water buffalo) which is diced and sun-dried until it hardens and loses most of its water content. The diced and dried skins are later fried in ample hot cooking oil until they expand similarly with bubbles and yield a crispy texture. This fried cattle skin is then sealed in vacuum plastic bags to ensure and prolong its crispiness."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-krupuk-kulit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krupuk_kulit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cowhide","ingredient/cowhide"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","